---
// How an item gets in, who publishes this, and what happens when it is wrong (issue #117): methodology, disclosure,
// how to cite, and the corrections log. Static, no script (AC12). Wording that depends on the data (labels, tiers,
// the manual-retrieval sentence) comes from the same constants the rest of the site uses, so the page can't drift
// from what item pages say.
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { Content as Disclosure } from '../owner/disclosure.md';
import { readCorrections, newestFirst, recordHref, reportReference } from '../lib/corrections.js';
import { SITE_URL, citationText, logOpenedText } from '../lib/methodology.js';
import {
  MANUAL_RETRIEVAL_TEXT,
  coverageText,
  dateText,
  easternText,
  labelText,
} from '../lib/radar.js';

const log = await readCorrections();
const entries = newestFirst(log.corrections);

const events = await getCollection('events');
const jurisdictions = await getCollection('jurisdictions');
const actions = await getCollection('actions');
const records = {
  events: new Set(events.map((e) => e.id)),
  jurisdictions: new Set(jurisdictions.map((j) => j.id)),
  actions: new Map(actions.map((a) => [a.id, a.data.jurisdiction_id])),
};
const names = new Map(jurisdictions.map((j) => [j.id, j.data.name]));

// The worked citation uses a real item: the latest confirmed one, else the latest of any label.
const byLatest = [...events].sort((a, b) => b.data.starts_utc.localeCompare(a.data.starts_utc));
const example =
  byLatest.find((e) => e.data.label === 'confirmed_official' && e.data.status === 'scheduled') ??
  byLatest[0];
const citation =
  example &&
  citationText({
    siteUrl: SITE_URL,
    id: example.id,
    title: example.data.title,
    jurisdiction: names.get(example.data.jurisdiction_id) ?? example.data.jurisdiction_id,
    startsText: easternText(example.data.starts_utc),
    document: {
      url: example.data.documents[0].url,
      retrievedText: easternText(example.data.documents[0].retrieved_utc),
      sha256: example.data.documents[0].sha256,
    },
  });
---

<Base title="Methodology and corrections">
  <h1>Methodology and corrections</h1>
  <p>
    How an item gets into this ledger, who publishes it, how to cite it, and what happens when
    something in it is wrong.
  </p>
  <nav aria-label="On this page">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="#how-items-get-in">How items get in</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#labels">What the labels mean</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#provenance">Where each item comes from</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#coverage">What is covered</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#publisher">Who publishes this</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#cite">How to cite</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#corrections">Corrections</a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="how-items-get-in">How items get in</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      For jurisdictions on an agenda platform we can read, a program checks for new agendas about
      once an hour. The scheduler sometimes skips a run, so the longest gap we measured between
      checks was just under 11 hours.
    </li>
    <li>
      When a check finds an agenda item that may concern a data center, it opens a review task in
      the same run. Nothing it finds is published at that point.
    </li>
    <li>
      Where the official site blocks programs, a person checks it instead. Those jurisdictions show
      as stale if they go 7 days without a check.
    </li>
    <li>
      A person reviews every item before it is public. They write the title in neutral words; the
      original agenda title and the names of petitioners and property owners are never published.
    </li>
    <li>
      Only reviewed records merged into the ledger are published; the site is built from nothing
      else. The site and its downloads are rebuilt after every merged change, and at least every 6
      hours.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="labels">What the labels mean</h2>
  <p>Every item carries exactly one of two labels.</p>
  <dl>
    <dt>{labelText('reported_news')}</dt>
    <dd>The item rests on a news report. We have not yet kept an official record that shows it.</dd>
    <dt>{labelText('confirmed_official')}</dt>
    <dd>
      At least one of the item's sources is an official record, such as an agenda, minutes, an
      ordinance or a case page, from the government's own site.
    </dd>
  </dl>
  <p>
    An item moves from reported to confirmed when we keep a copy of an official record that shows
    it. Another news report does not move it. When a label or anything else in a published item
    changes, the change shows up in the feed and calendars as an update.
  </p>
  <p>
    A decision shows only the terms, such as a start or end date, that one of its kept sources
    states. Some terms stay blank until the government posts the official text.
  </p>

  <h2 id="provenance">Where each item comes from</h2>
  <p>
    Every item lists its sources. For each one you see the address we took it from, when we took it
    (Eastern time), and the SHA-256 hash of the exact copy we kept. If you download the same file
    and its SHA-256 matches, you have the copy we used.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some official sites block automated downloads. A person then saves the file in a browser, and
    the item says: "{MANUAL_RETRIEVAL_TEXT}." The address, time and hash work the same way.
  </p>
  <p>
    We keep the documents in private storage and do not republish them. The source address is where
    to read them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="coverage">What is covered</h2>
  <p>Each jurisdiction's page states how closely it is covered:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>{coverageText('watched')}</li>
    <li>{coverageText('decisions')}</li>
    <li>{coverageText('registered')}</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If something is not in the ledger, that is not evidence it did not happen. A body can meet, hear
    or decide something we have not found or not yet recorded.
  </p>

  <h2 id="dates-ahead">Dates ahead</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/ahead/">Coming up on the record</a> lists two kinds of date, each only as an archived
    document in the decision's sources states it: a decision's expiry date, taken as the last day it
    is in force, and the date given for a matter's next step. We never count days, read a meeting
    schedule or use a news report's “expected” wording to make one. A changed date replaces the old
    one in calendars rather than adding another.
  </p>
  <p>
    After a date passes, it shows as recorded once the record shows what happened (for an expiry:
    expired, replaced or extended; for a next step: a meeting or decision of that body on that day).
    Until then it reads “Date passed. Nothing recorded for it yet.” That says only that our record
    hasn't caught up; it doesn't mean a moratorium lapsed or was renewed, or that a meeting did or
    didn't happen.
  </p>

  <h2 id="publisher">Who publishes this</h2>
  <div class="disclosure">
    <Disclosure />
  </div>

  <h2 id="cite">How to cite</h2>
  <p>
    Cite the item's page address, plus the source address, retrieval time and SHA-256 of the
    document you relied on. Item addresses do not change.
  </p>
  {citation && (
    <>
      <p>For example:</p>
      <blockquote class="citation">
        <p>{citation}</p>
      </blockquote>
    </>
  )}

  <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    To report a mistake, use the <a href="/report/">report form</a>. Say which item is wrong and, if
    you can, point to the record that shows it. Your email is optional; leave it if you want a
    reply.
  </p>
  <p>
    We act on every report within 48 hours: we correct the record, or, if you left an email address,
    we reply with what we found and why the record stands, or, when a fix depends on a record not
    yet posted, what we are waiting for and when. Every correction is listed below with the date it
    was reported and the date it was fixed. Entries are never removed.
  </p>

  <h3 id="log">Corrections log</h3>
  <p>{logOpenedText(log.opened_on)}</p>
  {entries.length === 0 ? (
    <p>No corrections yet.</p>
  ) : (
    <ol class="log" reversed>
      {entries.map((entry) => (
        <li id={`correction-${entry.id}`}>
          <p>
            <strong>
              Corrected <time datetime={entry.corrected_on}>{dateText(entry.corrected_on)}</time>.
            </strong>{' '}
            {entry.summary}
          </p>
          <p class="muted">
            Reported <time datetime={entry.reported_on}>{dateText(entry.reported_on)}</time>
            {entry.report !== null && <> as report {reportReference(entry.report)}</>}.
            {entry.record_ids.length > 0 && (
              <>
                {' '}
                Changed:{' '}
                {entry.record_ids.map((id, i) => (
                  <>
                    {i > 0 && ', '}
                    <a href={recordHref(id, records)}>
                      <code>{id}</code>
                    </a>
                  </>
                ))}
                .
              </>
            )}
          </p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  )}
</Base>
